PR #636  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Non-conforming interrupt handler entry (tpush not first) on Smtp hardware leads to speculative context corruption as implementations may pre-execute tpush before fetching the handler body.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:125-238  handle_exception
      Primary trap handler installed via CSR_TVEC has zero Smtp awareness. The first instruction is 'csrrw tp, CSR_SCRATCH, tp' (line 131), not 'tpush'. On a Smtp-enabled implementation that speculatively pre-executes tpush-on-trap before fetching the handler, the hardware would perform a phantom context push that the software never matches with tpopxret, causing a save/restore stack mismatch. The return path uses sret/mret (lines 315-316) instead of tpopxret. No tpush/tpopxret instructions exist anywhere in the kernel (grep confirmed zero hits).
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:203-206  .Lsetup_trap_vector
      Installs handle_exception as the trap vector via bare 'csrw CSR_TVEC, a0' using a raw function pointer. Smtp minor-identity requires the trap vector to use a minor-identity-aware table format (mivt/sivt) rather than a bare address. This setup writes a plain virtual address, which would fail to properly route traps per minor-identity context.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:419-419  (header define)
      Defines CSR_MTVEC=0x305 — a constant for the Machine Trap Vector CSR address. This is a passive #define of a hardware constant; it carries no Smtp-specific logic and is definitionally correct regardless of Smtp enablement.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:490-490  (header define)
      Defines CSR_TVEC as alias for CSR_MTVEC under CONFIG_RISCV_M_MODE. This is a passive alias for the M-mode path; any Smtp-aware TVEC manipulation through this alias would need to be handled at the write site, not the define.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:515-515  (header define)
      Defines CSR_TVEC as alias for CSR_STVEC under !CONFIG_RISCV_M_MODE (S-mode kernel). This is the path used by most real-world Linux kernels. A passive alias — Smtp awareness must be implemented at the csr_write/csr_read call sites, not at the #define level.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:449-458  do_irq
      C-level IRQ dispatcher called from handle_exception after context-save. No Smtp-specific interrupt filtering or minor-identity routing. The Smtp vulnerability is at the asm entry point (handle_exception), not the C dispatcher. do_irq itself is structurally fine — the missing tpush is upstream at the trap entry.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/suspend.c:15-42  suspend_save_csrs
      Saves CSR_TVEC via csr_read(CSR_TVEC) at line 19, storing the raw register value. Under Smtp with minor identities, the trap vector may point to a minor-identity-aware table (mivt/sivt) rather than a bare address. Saving only the raw tvec value is insufficient — the full minor-identity trap-vector table configuration must be preserved across suspend. Currently depends on the implicit invariant that Smtp is not enabled.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/suspend.c:44-60  suspend_restore_csrs
      Restores CSR_TVEC via csr_write(CSR_TVEC, context->tvec) at line 49. If Smtp minor-identity trap-vector tables were active before suspend, a raw csr_write of the saved base value would destroy the minor-identity table format, corrupting trap routing after resume. Currently safe because Smtp is not enabled, but the invariant is unchecked.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:330-334  secondary_start_common
      Secondary hart sets trap vector to .Lsecondary_park (debug spin loop) via bare csrw CSR_TVEC before setup_vm(). On a Smtp-enabled system, secondary harts must initialize their minor-identity trap-vector context before enabling interrupts. Currently depends on being non-Smtp hardware. Later calls .Lsetup_trap_vector at line 342 which also lacks Smtp awareness.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:84-87  _start
      Early boot: sets CSR_TVEC to a virtual address (la a2, 1f) to handle the MMU transition trap. This is a transient boot-time trap vector set with interrupts disabled and MMU off. No Smtp implications at this stage — the hart is not yet in a multi-tenant context and no minor identities exist.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:109-112  _start
      Early boot debug: sets trap vector to .Lsecondary_park (spin loop) after MMU transition. This is a debug/development trap handler set very early in boot with interrupts disabled. No Smtp risk at this stage.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:151-153  secondary_start_sbi
      Secondary hart SBI boot path: sets trap vector to .Lsecondary_park via bare csrw before paging setup. If Smtp were active, secondary harts need minor-identity trap-vector initialization before interrupts are enabled. No such initialization exists here or anywhere in the kernel.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:234-235  _start_kernel
      M-mode PMP setup: temporarily installs .Lpmp_done as the trap handler to skip PMP faults during bootstrap. This is a transient M-mode trap handler used before any meaningful trap infrastructure exists. Interrupts are masked (CSR_IE set to zero at line 219). No Smtp risk.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:370-373  .Lsecondary_start
      CONFIG_RISCV_BOOT_SPINWAIT path: secondary harts set trap vector to .Lsecondary_park while spin-waiting. The spinwait-to-boot transition on Smtp hardware would require the trap vector to handle minor-identity context switching during the handoff. No such handling exists.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_exit.c:65-75  kvm_riscv_vcpu_unpriv_read
      KVM temporarily redirects STVEC to __kvm_riscv_unpriv_trap via csr_swap(CSR_STVEC, ...) for unprivileged emulation, with interrupts disabled via local_irq_save. Saves/restores only the raw stvec value. Under Smtp, the host STVEC would point to a minor-identity trap-vector table. Swapping in a bare handler address destroys that table and restores only the raw old value. Currently safe because Smtp is not enabled, but this path would need to save/restore the full minor-identity mivt/sivt context.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_exit.c:125-125  kvm_riscv_vcpu_unpriv_read
      Restores STVEC via csr_write(CSR_STVEC, old_stvec) after KVM unprivileged emulation. Only the raw base address is restored. If the host STVEC was pointing to a Smtp minor-identity table, the restored value may not correctly reconstruct the full table context. The __kvm_riscv_unpriv_trap handler at vcpu_switch.S:250 also has no tpush/tpopxret — it would itself trigger the same Smtp violation if a trap occurred during the critical section.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:41-62  __kvm_riscv_switch_to
      SAVE_HOST_AND_RESTORE_GUEST_CSRS macro (VM entry): saves host STVEC via csrrw and redirects to VM resume path at line 51. Stores raw stvec value in host_stvec. Under Smtp, the saved host STVEC would need to encode the full minor-identity trap-vector table, not just a base address. The csrrw atomically reads the old value and writes the new — no tpush involved.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:140-152  __kvm_riscv_switch_to
      VM exit path (SAVE_GUEST_AND_RESTORE_HOST_CSRS): restores host STVEC via csrw CSR_STVEC, t0 at line 152. Loads the previously saved raw value from host_stvec. Cannot correctly reconstruct a Smtp minor-identity trap-vector table from a single raw address.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:580-594  kvm_arch_vcpu_load
      Writes CSR_VSTVEC and CSR_VSCAUSE to sync guest virtual trap state via nacl_csr_write (lines 588-591). Has zero awareness of Smtp minor-identity virtualized trap-vector tables (vmivt/vsivt). Guest Smtp minor-identity context cannot be virtualized through current KVM CSR save/restore infrastructure.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_host.h:108-114  (struct kvm_cpu_trap)
      struct kvm_cpu_trap stores sepc/scause/stval/htval/htinst for KVM trap forwarding. Has no minor_identity field and no mivt-related fields. Under Smtp, trap forwarding must preserve the minor-identity context that was active when the trap occurred, so the guest's minor-identity-aware handler receives the correct identity.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_host.h:156-168  (struct kvm_vcpu_csr)
      struct kvm_vcpu_csr stores vstvec/vscause/vsstatus etc. for KVM guest CSR context. Has no vmivt or vsivt fields for Smtp minor interrupt vector tables. Any Smtp minor-identity CSR virtualization would require adding fields here to save/restore the full minor-identity trap-vector table configuration per VCPU.

