PR #636  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Non-conforming interrupt handler entry (tpush not first) on Smtp hardware leads to speculative context corruption as implementations may pre-execute tpush before fetching the handler body.
  [BUG] firmware/fw_base.S:672-690  _trap_handler
      Primary M-mode trap handler. First executed instruction is csrrw tp, CSR_MSCRATCH, tp (from TRAP_SAVE_AND_SETUP_SP_T0 macro, line 491), not tpush. On Smtp hardware that speculatively pre-executes tpush before the handler is fetched, the shadow stack gets a push that the actual save sequence never accounts for. Every M-mode trap entry corrupts the shadow stack — the hardware-pushed entry has no matching pop, and the software save/restore sequence operates on the wrong shadow-stack depth.
  [BUG] firmware/fw_base.S:694-724  _trap_handler_hyp
      H-extension variant of the M-mode trap handler. Same TRAP_SAVE_AND_SETUP_SP_T0 macro expands to csrrw as first instruction (not tpush). Affects all harts with H-extension when Smtp is implemented. Identical shadow stack corruption mechanism as _trap_handler — speculative tpush pre-execution mismatches the actual save sequence.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_expected_trap.S:24-38  __sbi_expected_trap
      Temporary trap handler installed into MTVEC during CSR probing, unpriv emulation, and illegal atomic emulation. First instruction is 'li a4, MSTATUS_MPRV', not tpush. If any interrupt or exception fires while this handler is installed (window is brief but real), speculative tpush pre-execution mismatches the actual save sequence (which saves cause/tval to an a3-pointed struct). The handler ends with plain mret, leaving the speculatively-pushed shadow stack entry unconsumed.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_expected_trap.S:42-69  __sbi_expected_trap_hext
      H-extension variant of the temporary probe trap handler. Same violation: first instruction is 'li a4, MSTATUS_MPRV', not tpush. On Smtp-enabled harts with H-extension, any trap during the window when this handler is installed into MTVEC causes the same speculative shadow-stack mismatch as __sbi_expected_trap, with the added H-extension CSR save fields (MTVAL2, MTINST, GVA) also being saved without the hardware-expected shadow stack synchronization.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:830-832  sbi_hart_switch_mode
      Uses plain 'mret' to return to lower privilege mode. On Smtp, if the trap entry should have done tpush (which the hardware may have done speculatively), the matching exit instruction must be tpopxret — not mret. Plain mret leaves the speculatively-pushed shadow stack entry unconsumed, causing a persistent shadow-stack-depth leak. On repeated traps (e.g., timer interrupts + ecalls), the shadow stack eventually overflows, causing either spurious faults or silent wrap-around corruption.
  [BUG] firmware/fw_base.S:393-401  _start_warm
      This is the definitive MTVEC configuration site for normal operation. Installs _trap_handler (or _trap_handler_hyp for H-extension) into CSR_MTVEC. Neither handler begins with tpush. This configuration alone guarantees that every M-mode trap on a Smtp implementation will trigger the speculative-shadow-stack-mismatch bug. This is the architectural root cause — all other handler violations stem from this configuration point.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:304-377  sbi_trap_handler
      This C-level dispatch function reads regs->mepc, regs->mstatus, trap->cause, etc. from the sbi_trap_context populated by the assembly entry points. If the assembly save sequence was corrupted by a tpush speculative mismatch (shadow stack pointer misaligned, or register save slots shifted), all downstream decisions — ecall dispatch, trap redirect, SSE injection — operate on garbage register state. The C code itself is correct, but it implicitly trusts that the assembly entry sequence produced a consistent trap context, which is NOT guaranteed on Smtp hardware.
  [BUG] lib/utils/serial/semihosting.c:62-71  semihosting_enabled
      Temporarily installs _semihost_test_vector (a 3-instruction handler doing mepc+=4; mret) into MTVEC during semihosting probe. This handler does not begin with tpush. On a Smtp implementation, if any interrupt fires while this temporary MTVEC is active, speculative tpush would mismatch the handler's simple mret, overwriting a shadow stack entry. When the real handler is later restored, the shadow stack is one entry deeper than expected.
  [BUG] platform/generic/thead/thead_c9xx_tlb_trap_handler.S:10-13  _thead_tlb_flush_fixup_trap_handler
      Platform-specific TLB-fixup trap handler installed into MTVEC. First instruction is 'sfence.vma zero, t0', not tpush. After the sfence.vma, it chains into _trap_handler (which also lacks tpush). On Smtp hardware, speculative tpush occurs before sfence.vma executes, corrupting the shadow stack. The sfence.vma itself doesn't interact with the shadow stack, but the mismatched push depth means _trap_handler's register save layout is offset from where the hardware expects it.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:148-151  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Domain context switch saves and restores sbi_trap_context via sbi_memcpy. If the in-memory trap context was corrupted at entry due to missing tpush in the assembly handler, this memcpy faithfully propagates the corrupted register state (mepc, mstatus, general regs) to the target domain's saved context. When the target domain later resumes, it restores corrupted architectural state. The domain switch code is itself correct, but it has no way to detect or reject an already-corrupted trap context.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_sse.c:553-557  sse_event_inject
