PR #636  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Non-conforming interrupt handler entry (tpush not first) on Smtp hardware leads to speculative context corruption as implementations may pre-execute tpush before fetching the handler body.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:208-217  cpu_exception_handler
      This is the primary trap vector installed in stvec. On a Smtp-enabled implementation, the hardware would speculatively pre-execute tpush before this handler is fetched, expecting tpush to be the very first instruction. Instead, the first instruction is csrrw sp, sscratch, sp. If Smtp were ever enabled (via ISA string detection and CSR configuration), this entry would cause a speculative save/restore mismatch: the hardware's pre-executed tpush would push context expecting a matching hardware pop, but the handler's manual save_registers macro would duplicate the save, leading to stack corruption or register corruption. Currently safe only because Smtp is entirely absent from the codebase (no ISA detection, no CSR definitions, no enable bits). The invariant 'Smtp is disabled' is true by omission, not by explicit guard.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:219-225  cpu_exception_handler_supervisor
      Supervisor-mode interrupt handler entry point. First instruction is save_registers 1 (expands to addi sp, sp, -(TF_SIZE)). On a Smtp-enabled implementation, the speculative pre-executed tpush would have already saved context to the stack; this manual save_registers would then write a second trap frame, corrupting the hardware-saved state and causing incorrect register restoration on sret. Same implicit-safety condition as cpu_exception_handler: Smtp is never detected nor enabled.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/exception.S:227-235  cpu_exception_handler_user
      User-mode trap handler entry point. Same problem: first instruction is save_registers 0 (manual stack-based save), but Smtp hardware would have pre-executed tpush. The double-save would corrupt the trap frame. Additionally, this handler includes do_ast before load_registers, meaning any AST-induced context changes could interact destructively with hardware-managed Smtp state. Safe only because Smtp is unimplemented.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_switch.S:206-220  vmm_unpriv_trap
      VMM auxiliary trap vector temporarily installed in stvec during guest memory access emulation (hlvx.hu). First instruction is csrr a1, sepc — not tpush. On a Smtp-enabled implementation, the speculative tpush would save VMM context to an unexpected stack location. Additionally, this handler modifies sepc (addi a1, a1, 4; csrw sepc, a1) to skip the faulting hlvx.hu, but Smtp hardware state management may not track sepc overwrites correctly, potentially desynchronizing the hardware's trap-push/pop state machine. Safe only because Smtp is unimplemented.


